          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Operatic Ambitions</strong><br>\nBeyond ragtime, Joplin aspired to produce operas that combined his musical roots with classical traditions. His opera \"Treemonisha\" was not well received at the time but is now considered an important work that reflects his ambitious vision for African American music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Music Publishing and Teaching</strong><br>\nJoplin spent much of his career in Missouri, where he published his compositions and taught aspiring musicians. His work as a teacher and mentor helped to establish a foundation for the ragtime community and ensured the dissemination of his music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Struggle for Recognition</strong><br>\nDespite his success, Joplin faced racial discrimination and financial difficulties. The recognition and rewards for his music were limited due to the social constraints of the time, which affected his personal and professional life.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
