          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence on Later Music</strong><br>\nJoplin's influence extended beyond his lifetime as his music went on to affect the development of jazz and other musical genres. The rhythmic patterns and melodies of ragtime found a place in the evolving tapestry of American music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Final Years and Decline</strong><br>\nIn his final years, Joplin suffered from syphilis, which led to a decline in his health and financial status. He continued to compose, but his later works did not gain the popularity of his earlier compositions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Fame</strong><br>\nScott Joplin died on April 1, 1917, in New York City. His music was largely forgotten until a ragtime revival in the 1970s restored his status as a pioneering American musician. Today, Joplin's work is celebrated worldwide for its rhythmic brilliance and historical importance.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
